import {
  "moonbitlang/async/js_async",
}

///| Loop #52: where a TOFU write-back actually goes.
///
///|
///
/// Loops #49 and #50 left a gap that read as a decision and was really a
/// deferral. The write-back was **printed**, and the gate captured the line and
/// fed it back by hand. That is fine for a test and useless in the product: in a
/// browser there is nobody to read stdout, so `--kh-tofu` on a second visit
/// starts from nothing again. The pin is remembered for the length of one test
/// run.
///
///|
///
/// The shape is the one `src/knownhosts/render.mbt` established -- "what a line
/// looks like" lives in `src/knownhosts` and is shared by both transports; "where
/// it goes" is the platform's business. What was missing is that the platform side
/// had no implementation at all.
///
///|
///
/// **js target only, and deliberately not gated at the package level.** Same
/// reasoning as `src/wt`: a `targets:` gate on the package would block downstream
/// consumers, so the gating is per file. `store.mbt` uses `extern "js"`, which the
/// wasm backends reject outright, so a wasm-wanting consumer fails loudly at its
/// own compile rather than silently here.
///
///|
///
/// The extern bodies use the double-quoted IIFE form rather than `#| ... |#`. The
/// compiler evaluates the right-hand side as a module constant, and the inline form
/// leaked its own terminator into the emitted JavaScript and produced a SyntaxError
/// at load time in the browser -- see `src/wt/ffi.mbt`, which found that the hard
/// way.
///
///|
///
/// **Never throw.** Every failure is a value. A storage shim that raised would
/// turn "the browser is in private mode" into a failed connection, which is the
/// wrong failure for the wrong reason: the connection itself is fine, and the user
/// loses the ability to pin for a session rather than losing the session.
options(
  targets: { "store.mbt": [ "js" ] },
)